# Build products. The Makefile writes binaries into the repo root and stages
# them to /tmp/nw-init-run; none of it belongs in git.
#
# Patterns, not a name list: this file previously enumerated each binary and
# fell behind twice when new targets were added (nw-dawn and unit-term).
# Every TCB binary is nw-* and every fixture is unit-*.
#
# ANCHORED TO THE ROOT, because the unanchored form was false in the way
# this repository specialises in. The comment here read "every source is
# nwcheck.c / nwspawn.c / nwsup.c / unit_probe.c -- underscores, no hyphen
# -- so these patterns catch the products and no source", and bare `nw-*`
# matches a basename at any depth, so it caught `bakery/nw-cc.py`: a source,
# the baker, which invariant 3 makes atomic with `blob.h`, `plan.als` and
# `Plan.tla`. Nothing broke,
# because a tracked file stays tracked and `git check-ignore` does not even
# report one without --no-index -- so the only symptom was that a fresh
# `git init && git add -A` silently dropped it. Found when a control fixture
# built that way came up one code file short.
#
# The Makefile writes its products into the repo root, so the root is where
# the patterns belong.
*.o
/nw-*
/unit-*
# tools/mkboot.sh's output: a source copy, a static build, an initrd, and two
# 64 MiB disk images. Rebuilt from scratch on every run (the ESP is dd'd from
# /dev/zero), so nothing in it is worth tracking and one `make qemu` would
# otherwise stage ~130 MB.
boot-out/
.reviews/
*.gcov
*.gcno
*.gcda
__pycache__/
specs/limits.als
specs/Plan.cfg
# TLC/Alloy leave a states/ dir and a per-command output dir beside the spec
states/
